
==============================================================================
S1  ord_9(2)=6 <=> 9 | 2^6-1 = 63 : rows, exponent classes, R-period, factor 7
==============================================================================
2^k mod 9, k=0..11 : [1, 2, 4, 8, 7, 5, 1, 2, 4, 8, 7, 5]
PROVED: ord_9(2)=6, i.e. 2^6-1=63 is the first Mersenne number divisible by 9;
        2 is a primitive root mod 9: <2> = {1,2,4,8,7,5} = (Z/9)^*.
2^k mod 63, k=0..6 : [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 1]  ord_63(2) = 6
2^k mod  7, k=0..6 : [1, 2, 4, 1, 2, 4, 1]   ord_7(2)  = 3
PROVED: ord_63(2)=lcm(ord_9(2),ord_7(2))=lcm(6,3)=6 (already in THM-4139 (38));
        <2> mod 63 = {1,2,4,8,16,32} projects ISOMORPHICALLY onto (Z/9)^* and 2:1 onto <2> mod 7 = {1,2,4}.
PROVED (+checked odd n<100001): F(6j+1)=9j+2, F(6j+3)=9j+5, F(6j+5)=9j+8; every image is 2 mod 3.
   Mechanism: 3n+1 mod 18 is a function of n mod 6; halving by 2^{-1}=5 mod 9 sends {4,10,16} mod 18 to {2,5,8} mod 9.
   This uses only the multiplier 3 and 2^{-1} = 2 mod 3; it does NOT use ord_9(2)=6.

powers of two in the image rows (k = exponent, 2^k = F(n) needs 2^k = 2 mod 3, i.e. k odd):
   k mod 6 | 2^k mod 9 = image row b | source row r=(2^{k+1}-1)/3 mod 6 | 2^k mod 7
   1       | 2                     | 1                                | 2
   3       | 8                     | 5                                | 1
   5       | 5                     | 3                                | 4
PROVED: 2^k lies in image row b iff k is odd and k mod 6 = 1 (b=2, row 1), 5 (b=5, row 3), 3 (b=8, row 5).
   Mechanism: k odd is the gate 2^k = 2 mod 3 (ord_3(2)=2); which row is k mod 6 (ord_9(2)=6).
   The factor 7 sees k mod 3 (ord_7(2)=3): on odd k, k mod 6 -> k mod 3 is a bijection, so 7 sees the
   row braid too, but NOT the parity gate.  3 sees only the gate.  9 = 3^2 sees both.
PROVED (sympy symbolic): R^3(n) = 64n+21 = 63n + (n+21);  R^3(n)-n = 63n+21 = 21(3n+1) = 3*7*(3n+1); 21 = 63/3.
PROVED (+checked |n|<=2000 odd): R adds 4 mod 6, so rows cycle 1->5->3->1 with exact period 3;
   9 | 4^t-1 iff 3 | t (v_3(4^t-1)=1+v_3(t)), so the row period 3 IS ord_9(4)=3, i.e. ord_9(2)=6.
   R^3 fixes the SOURCE modulo 42 = 6*7 and the TARGET F(n) modulo 63 = 9*7 (F(R^3 n) = 64 F(n)).
   R itself multiplies the target by 4, of order 3 in both (Z/9)^* and (Z/7)^*.
   42 is the EXACT universal source modulus: R^3(n)-n = 21(3n+1) is never 0 mod 126 (3 !| 3n+1) and is
   0 mod 84 iff n = 1 mod 4 (checked |n|<=2000 odd).
R mod 7 (x -> 4x+1) orbits: [(0, 1, 5), (2,), (3, 4, 6)]
PROVED: R mod 7 has the single fixed class n = 2 mod 7 (exactly the sources with 7 | 3n+1, i.e. 7 | F(n))
        and two 3-cycles {0,1,5}, {3,4,6}; so R has period 3 mod 6, mod 7, hence mod 42, but period 9 mod 18.
target classes mod 63 containing powers of two: [2, 8, 32]  (2^1, 2^3, 2^5) -> mod 9: [2, 8, 5]  mod 7: [2, 1, 4]
PROVED: exactly 3 of the 21 target classes mod 63 contain powers of two, one per row mod 9 (2,8,5) and
        one per class {2,1,4} mod 7; the rows mod 9 are the mod-63 target classes modulo the factor 7
        in the exact sense (Z/63)^* = (Z/9)^* x (Z/7)^*, 2 -> (2,2), and <2> -> (Z/9)^* is an isomorphism.

general odd p: rows of pn+1 are the p odd classes mod 2p; powers of two in the image rows occupy
   ord_{p^2}(2)/ord_p(2) distinct rows (all of them iff p is non-Wieferich):
   p    | ord_p(2) | ord_{p^2}(2) | #rows hit by powers of two | target classes mod p^2 (= 2^{-1} mod p), in bijection with the p odd source classes mod 2p
   3    | 2        | 6            | 3                          | [2, 5, 8]
   5    | 4        | 20           | 5                          | [3, 8, 13, 18, 23]
   7    | 3        | 21           | 7                          | [4, 11, 18, 25, 32, 39, 46]
   11   | 10       | 110          | 11                         | [6, 17, 28, 39, 50, 61, 72, 83, 94, 105, 116]
   13   | 12       | 156          | 13                         | [7, 20, 33, 46, 59, 72, 85, 98, 111, 124, 137, 150, 163]
   1093 | 364      | 364          | 1                          | single class 597325 mod 1194649
   3511 | 1755     | 1755         | 1                          | single class 6163561 mod 12327121
PROVED: the power-of-two exponent classes are the cosets of ord_p(2) inside ord_{p^2}(2); the count is p
        iff 2^{ord_p(2)} != 1 mod p^2 (non-Wieferich).  REFUTED as a universal law: p=1093, 3511 put every
        power of two in the image into ONE row.  For p=3 the count 3 is 6/2: '3 rows see 3 exponent classes'.
   Wieferich inverse braid: least q=2^d with p | q-1 has r = v_p(q-1) = {1093: 'd=364, r=2', 3511: 'd=1755, r=2'}
PROVED: for p=1093 (d=364) and p=3511 (d=1755) r=2, so by inherited summand.md (11) the inverse braid R_p=qn+c
        has period p^{max(0,s-1)} on odd classes mod 2p^s: NOT full (fixes the first base-p digit).  A visible degeneration.

==============================================================================
S2  Odd-multiplier tower F_p = (pn+1)/2, summand typing, inverse braids R_p
==============================================================================
PROVED: F_{p+2}(n) = F_p(n) + n for all odd p, n; hence F_p(n) = F_1(n) + ((p-1)/2) n with F_1(n)=(n+1)/2.
   Summand typing (inherited summand.md section 1,3): the arrow n -> F_p(n) in the strict summand shadow has
   the unique companion F_p(n) - n = F_{p-2}(n).  p=3: companion F_1(n)=(n+1)/2 (the inherited witness (a,b,z));
   p=5: the companion of n in 5n+1 is its own 3n+1 image F_3(n);  p=1: companion F_{-1}(n) = (1-n)/2 <= 0,
   so the n+1 map (n+1)/2 is NOT a strict positive summand arrow (its companion is nonpositive) for n>=1.
PROVED: F_{p-2}(n) = n  <=>  (p-4) n = -1  <=>  p-4 is a unit  <=>  (p,n) in {(3,1), (5,-1)} over odd integers of
        either sign; for n>=1 only (p,n)=(3,1), the trivial Collatz cycle 1 -> 2 (checked p<40, n<400: [(3, 1)];
        signed search odd p in [-9,39], odd |n|<1000: [(3, 1), (5, -1)]; F_3(-1)=(-3+1)/2=-1, an equal-summand arrow of 5n+1).

inverse-fibre braids R_p(n) = q n + c, q = least power of two = 1 mod p, c=(q-1)/p (inherited summand.md sec.5):
   p | q=2^d | c  | r=v_p(q-1) | F_p(R_p n)=q F_p(n) | period of R_p on odd classes mod 2p, 2p^2, 2p^3 | height shift d
   1 | 2     | 1  | n/a        | True                | [1, 1, 1]                                      | 1
   3 | 4     | 1  | 1          | True                | [3, 9, 27]                                     | 2
   5 | 16    | 3  | 1          | True                | [5, 25, 125]                                   | 4
   7 | 8     | 1  | 1          | True                | [7, 49, 343]                                   | 3
   11| 1024  | 93 | 1          | True                | [11, 121, 1331]                                | 10
   13| 4096  | 315 | 1          | True                | [13, 169, 2197]                                | 12
PROVED (inherited (10)-(11)): for p=3,5,7,11,13 r=1 so R_p is ONE cycle on odd classes mod 2p^s of length p^s;
   the row period equals p (3 for 3n+1, 5 for 5n+1, 7 for 7n+1).  The height shift per braid step is d=ord_p(2).
   p=1: q=2, c=1, R_1(n)=2n+1, F_1(R_1 n) = 2 F_1(n).  The fibre of core u under F_1 is n=2^{h+1}u-1 for EVERY h>=0
   (no congruence gate, no parity of h, height shift 1): R_1 = tau o D o tau^{-1} with tau(x)=x-1, D(x)=2x,
   i.e. the p=1 'braid' is the inherited doubling forest itself, shifted by one.  Its modulus 2*1^s is 2, so
   the odometer statement is empty; on odd classes mod 6 R_1 has orbits (1,3) and (5): NO single row cycle.

FINITE-EXACT positive cycles of the odd-to-odd maps T_p (starts n<=20000 odd, <=3000 steps, values capped 10^40):
   p=1: cycles found = [(1,)] ; starts not resolved within bounds = 0
   p=3: cycles found = [(1,)] ; starts not resolved within bounds = 0
   p=5: cycles found = [(1, 3), (13, 33, 83), (17, 43, 27)] ; starts not resolved within bounds = 9605
   p=7: cycles found = [(1,)] ; starts not resolved within bounds = 9982
   PROVED: T_1 converges for every n>=1 since (n+1)/2 < n for n>1.  Others: FINITE-EXACT only (unresolved starts
   are divergence candidates within the cap, not proofs).

