==============================================================================
S5  Typing table: every 'three' and 'two' in the prompt; the exact identity c(c+1)^2 = -63/64 at c=-7/4
==============================================================================
2x+1 iterated from 0 gives 2^n-1.  Terms with NO new prime (gcd-stripping, exact):
   n in 2..64 with no new prime: [6]   (63 = 3^2 * 7, 3 | 2^2-1, 7 | 2^3-1)
   CITED: Bang (1886)/Zsigmondy (1892): 2^n-1 has a primitive prime divisor for every n except n=1 and n=6.
   FINITE-EXACT here for n<=64.

x^2+c from 0, first terms; which terms introduce no new prime in the NUMERATOR:
   c=-7/4   : -7/4, 21/16, -7/256, -114639/65536 ... ; no-new-prime at terms [3] (of 2..6)
   c=-29/16 : -29/16, 377/256, 23345/65536, -7239639199/4294967296 ... ; no-new-prime at terms [] (of 2..6)
   c=-1/4   : -1/4, -3/16, -55/256, -13359/65536 ... ; no-new-prime at terms [] (of 2..6)
   c=-1     : -1, 0, -1, 0 ... ; no-new-prime at terms [2, 3, 4, 5, 6] (of 2..6)
